      m_Calls: []
  m_text: During the 1980s, Honda set out to create a supercar that could compete
    with European exotics while remaining reliable and comfortable enough for everyday
    driving. The result was the NSX, introduced in 1990. It was the world's first
    mass-production car with an all-aluminum monocoque body, reducing weight while
    maintaining structural strength. Formula 1 champion Ayrton Senna tested development
    prototypes and provided feedback that contributed to improvements in chassis
    rigidity. The NSX used a mid-mounted 3.0L VTEC V6 and was sold in North America
    as the Acura NSX. Its combination of performance, reliability, and usability
    changed expectations for modern supercars.
