      m_Calls: []
  m_text: The Skyline GT-R name first appeared in 1969 with the first-generation
    "Hakosuka" GT-R, which became successful in Japanese motorsport. After disappearing
    for more than a decade, the GT-R returned with the R32 in 1989. The R34 GT-R
    followed in 1999 and became the final Skyline GT-R generation. It used Nissan's
    2.6L twin-turbo RB26DETT engine and ATTESA E-TS all-wheel-drive system, while
    its famous dashboard display allowed drivers to monitor vehicle performance data.
    Video games such as Gran Turismo and films such as The Fast and the Furious helped
    turn the R34 into a global automotive icon.
